---

## Context

This skill is the single touchpoint between a host-project agent and the brain
layer. It plays two roles in one prompt: a general-purpose interface for
brain reads/writes during normal work, and a wiring flow that runs the first
time the brain is hit in a fresh project (or when the user repoints at a
different vault). The `/aide` orchestrator does not duplicate any wiring
logic — when the boot reporter says the brain is unwired or drifted, the
orchestrator routes here. Anything this prompt teaches the agent to do is
canonical; anything it omits or contradicts is canonical too, by negation.

The package recently underwent two cascading refactors that the prompt has
not yet absorbed: a frontmatter schema collapse (down to just `name` and
`mcpServerConfig`), and a hub-artifact sourcing change (artifact bytes now
flow from `brain.aide` body sections, not from inline TypeScript constants).
Three concrete contracts changed under the prompt as a result. The
`BrainState` shape returned by the boot reporter no longer carries
`vaultPath`, `rootPath`, `connector`, `entryFile`, or `tools` — only
`status`, `name`, and `hints`. The `brain.aide` file now lives at
`.aide/config/brain.aide`, in a user-owned directory the install/upgrade
tooling treats as immutable after first scaffold; the legacy `.aide/brain.aide`
location is gone. And the boot status set is now exactly four states — `ok`,
`no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, `mcp-drift` — with the previous
`invalid-path` state retired alongside `rootPath`. Any prose that still
references the pre-collapse model teaches the agent to look at fields,
files, and states the package no longer exposes.

The wiring flow is shaped by a single architectural tension. The MCP server
must remain read-only against `.mcp.json` — silent rewrites by anything
running inside the MCP runtime are the regression class the visible-command
boundary exists to prevent (root intent #14). At the same time, forcing the
user to leave the conversation and type a sync command in a separate terminal
is gratuitous friction without any contract protection in return: the
sanctioned mutation surface is the sync binary itself, not the human typing
it. The resolution is to have the agent invoke the sync CLI through the Bash
tool. The CLI binary stays the only mutation surface; the MCP server still
never writes `.mcp.json`; the user's pipeline is not interrupted by a
context switch. The launcher invocation for sync is recovered from
`.mcp.json` at runtime so the same instructions work uniformly for prod
installs (`cmd /c npx @aidemd-mcp/server sync`) and local-dev installs
(`node <path>/dist/index.js sync`).

One manual step remains and cannot be eliminated. Claude Code reads
`.mcp.json` only at session startup; the brain MCP server in the running
session was launched before any brain entry existed, and nothing the agent
does mid-session can hot-reload it. After the agent runs sync, the user must
restart Claude Code so the brain MCP server relaunches with the freshly-
written `.mcp.json` brain entry. The prompt's job is to tell the user this
once, explain why, and then stop — not to keep checking state in a session
where the live brain server is provably stale.

## Strategy

**Read only the fields the post-collapse `BrainState` actually carries.**
The boot reporter's tagged union exposes `status`, `name`, and `hints` — and
nothing else. Every other field that previously existed on this type
(`vaultPath`, `rootPath`, `connector`, `entryFile`, `tools`) was deleted in
the collapse refactor (handoff intent #11 + retired-fields list). The Step 1
"Gather state" prose must name those three fields and stop. Naming any
retired field teaches the agent the pre-collapse model, which silently
recreates the path-validation regression the schema collapse removed; the
parser will not even populate those fields, so the agent will read undefined
and branch wrong.

**Branch on exactly four states, one branch per state, with no merging.**
The boot reporter surfaces `ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, and
`mcp-drift` (handoff intent #12). The retired `invalid-path` state went
away with `rootPath` — there is no path the package validates anymore.
`no-mcp-entry` and `mcp-drift` happen to route through the same agent-
invoked sync call, but they are distinct states with distinct meanings:
`no-mcp-entry` means `.mcp.json` has no brain key at all (sync writes a
fresh one), `mcp-drift` means the brain key exists but its `command`/`args`
disagree with `mcpServerConfig` in `brain.aide` (sync overwrites it). The
state table must name both by their distinct values rather than collapsing
them into one generic "out of sync" branch — the user-facing prose can
explain what each state means in terms the user can act on.

**The brain config path is `.aide/config/brain.aide`, everywhere.** This
location is the user-owned-config contract (handoff intent #1 plus the
`.aide/config/` ownership boundary in root intent). The pre-collapse
`.aide/brain.aide` path is gone and the package no longer scaffolds it.
Any prose that references the retired path — branch text, error messages,
example blocks — points the agent at a file the package will never write
or read, and the user-owned-config invariant cannot be honored against a
location that does not exist.

**Scaffolding is one `aide_init` call, treated as both scaffold and done.**
The post-collapse `provisionBrain` pipeline is a single planning pass that
sources hub artifacts from `brain.aide` body sections (handoff intent #6)
and runs the five steps internally (config → root dirs → playbook hub →
research hub → MCP entry). The previous "Step 4 — Seed the vault directories"
phase issued a second `aide_init` call to seed brain content from a separate
template; that seam was deliberately removed by the hub-artifact sourcing
refactor. The prompt must drive exactly one `aide_init` call on the
`no-brain-aide` branch and not chain a follow-up seeding step.

**Sync is invoked from the agent's Bash tool, with the launcher recovered
from `.mcp.json` at runtime.** The recipe is uniform: read
`.mcp.json["mcpServers"]["aide"]` to recover the launcher's `command` and
`args` (this is the launcher Claude Code itself uses to start the AIDE MCP
server in the current session, so it is guaranteed to exist and to work),
append `"sync"` as the final argument, and run via Bash. This works
byte-identically for prod installs (where the recovered launcher is
something like `cmd /c npx @aidemd-mcp/server`) and local-dev installs
(where it is `node D:/.../dist/index.js`). Hardcoding either invocation
inline in the prompt breaks the other case, and breaks any custom binary
path a user might have. The launcher is recovered, never named.

**Sync stays a CLI verb; promoting it to an MCP tool is forbidden.** The
sync binary is the only sanctioned mutation surface for `.mcp.json` (root
intent invariant + handoff intent #14). Adding an `aide_sync` MCP tool
would route mutation through the MCP runtime, which is exactly the
silent-rewrite class of failure the visible-command boundary exists to
prevent. The agent invoking the binary via Bash preserves the boundary —
the mutation still runs through the binary, with output the user can see
in the conversation transcript. Direct `.mcp.json` writes from the agent,
in-session JSON merges, MCP-tool wrappers that "fix" drift — all carry
the same regression and are out.

**After sync, ask the user to restart Claude Code, explain why, and STOP.**
Claude Code re-reads `.mcp.json` only at session boot (a hard property of
the host, not a package decision); the brain MCP server in the running
session was launched without the brain entry, so disk state and live-server
state diverge until the host relaunches. The prompt names the constraint —
this is the one step the agent genuinely cannot do on the user's behalf —
so the user understands it as a host limit rather than a ritual. The
prompt then stops. It does not re-call `aide_info` to "confirm" `ok`,
because the in-session brain MCP wasn't reloaded and the check would
report disk state, not live-server state, producing false confidence.
Continuation happens on the next `/aide` invocation after restart.

**`mcp-drift` is handled identically to the post-scaffold path.** The disk
state is correct, just `.mcp.json` is behind, so the agent runs sync via
Bash and asks for restart. The prompt does not punt to "go run sync in
your terminal" prose — the user already invoked `/aide`, the agent is
holding the same binary the user would invoke, and forcing the context
switch buys no contract protection (the sanctioned surface is the binary,
not the human typing it). The two-step "run sync, then restart" manual
remediation is reduced to one step: the restart, the only step the agent
can't perform.

**Mode 1 (general brain interaction) is preserved unchanged in spirit.**
The skill remains the general-purpose brain interface — `aide_brain` first,
defer to the entry-point file's navigation rules, never hardcode a backend
tool name. Edits to Mode 1 are scoped to keeping its prose consistent with
the post-collapse contract (terminology aligned with the four-state model,
references to `.aide/config/brain.aide` rather than the legacy path), not
to changing its behavior. The `BrainToolResult` shape returned by
`aide_brain` is just `status` and `instructions` — the prompt's Mode 1
instructions to "execute those instructions verbatim" remain correct as
written.

## Good examples

A correct "Step 1 — Gather state" passage names exactly the fields the
post-collapse `BrainState` actually returns:

> Call `aide_info` to read `brain.status`, `brain.name`, and `brain.hints`.
> Branch on `brain.status`. The four states the boot reporter can return
> are `ok`, `no-brain-aide`, `no-mcp-entry`, and `mcp-drift` — handle each
> in its own branch below.

A correct status branch table covers the four states with one branch each
and routes the two sync-required states through the same agent-driven
recipe:

> - **`ok`** — brain is wired and `.mcp.json` is in sync. Tell the user
>   there's nothing to do. If `$ARGUMENTS` carries a path different from
>   the configured one, treat that as a re-point and continue to Step 2.
>   Otherwise **STOP**.
>
> - **`no-brain-aide`** — `.aide/config/brain.aide` does not exist.
>   Continue to Step 2 to scaffold it.
>
> - **`no-mcp-entry`** — `.aide/config/brain.aide` exists but `.mcp.json`
>   has no `brain` key. Skip scaffolding and run sync from Step 4.
>
> - **`mcp-drift`** — `.aide/config/brain.aide` exists and `.mcp.json` has
>   a `brain` key, but its `command`/`args` disagree with
>   `mcpServerConfig` in `brain.aide`. Skip scaffolding and run sync from
>   Step 4.

A correct sync invocation passage recovers the launcher rather than
hardcoding it:

> Read `.mcp.json` and find the `mcpServers["aide"]` entry — that's the
> launcher Claude Code itself uses to start the AIDE MCP server in this
> session, so it is guaranteed to exist. Take its `command` and `args`,
> append the literal string `"sync"` as a final argument, and run the
> resulting command via the Bash tool. For a typical prod install this
> will look like `cmd /c npx @aidemd-mcp/server sync`; for a local-dev
> install it will look like `node D:/path/to/dist/index.js sync`. The
> recipe is the same — recover the launcher, append `"sync"`, run.

A correct post-sync user message names the constraint the user is being
asked to honor and stops cleanly:

> Sync wrote the brain entry into `.mcp.json` successfully.
>
> Restart Claude Code so the brain MCP server picks up the new entry.
> Claude Code only reads `.mcp.json` at session startup — the brain MCP
> server in the current session was launched before the entry existed, so
> it can't be hot-reloaded mid-session. After the restart, re-run
> `/aide` and we'll continue where we left off.

Then the prompt **STOPS**. It does not call `aide_info` again. It does
not continue the user's original request.

## Bad examples

A "Step 1 — Gather state" passage that names retired fields:

> Call `aide_info` to read `brain.status`, `brain.vaultPath`, and
> `brain.hints`. Branch on `brain.status`...

`brain.vaultPath` does not exist on the post-collapse `BrainState` — that
field went away with `rootPath` and the `invalid-path` state. The agent
will read undefined and either branch on garbage or hit the
non-existent-state branch. The same pattern with `brain.connector`,
`brain.entryFile`, or `brain.tools` fails identically.

A status branch table that includes `invalid-path`:

> - **`invalid-path`** — `brain.aide` exists but `rootPath` is empty or
>   doesn't resolve on disk. Tell the user to fix the path and re-run...

The `invalid-path` state was retired alongside `rootPath` in the schema
collapse — there is no path the package validates anymore (path
validation is the launcher's problem at MCP server startup). A branch
matching this state will never fire, and its presence teaches the agent
the pre-collapse model.

A status branch table that punts `mcp-drift` to a separate terminal:

> - **`mcp-drift`** — `brain.aide` is configured correctly but
>   `.mcp.json` is out of sync. Run `npx aidemd-mcp sync` in your
>   terminal to apply the config, then restart Claude Code and re-run
>   `/aide`.

This is the regression that triggered this whole spec. The agent has
Bash and the same binary the user would invoke; forcing the user to
context-switch buys no contract protection (the sanctioned mutation
surface is the binary, not the human typing it).

A "Step 4 — Seed the vault directories" that issues a second `aide_init`:

> Call `aide_init({ category: "brain", brainPath: <brainPath> })` to seed
> vault directories. Brain steps are seed-semantic — `"would-create"`
> (applied silently by the tool) or `"exists"` (already present). The
> tool writes directories and seed files itself.

This duplicates work the post-collapse `provisionBrain` pipeline already
handles in a single planning pass that sources hub artifacts from
`brain.aide` body sections. The seam this step lived in was deliberately
removed by the hub-artifact-sourcing refactor; reintroducing it
re-creates exactly the duplication the refactor eliminated.

A reference to the legacy `.aide/brain.aide` path:

> Open `.aide/brain.aide`, correct `rootPath` to an absolute path that
> exists on disk, save the file, then re-run `/aide`.

The current canonical location is `.aide/config/brain.aide` — the
package no longer scaffolds, reads, or writes the legacy path. Pointing
the user at the wrong file fails silently (they edit a file the package
will never read).

A proposal to add `aide_sync` as an MCP tool:

> Add an `aide_sync` MCP tool the agent can call directly to update
> `.mcp.json` without shelling out.

Routes `.mcp.json` mutation through the MCP runtime, which is exactly
the silent-rewrite class of failure the visible-command boundary exists
to prevent (root intent #14). The CLI binary remains the only sanctioned
mutation surface; the agent invokes it via Bash.

An agent that reads `brain.aide` directly and writes `.mcp.json` itself:

> Read `.aide/config/brain.aide`, parse the `mcpServerConfig`, merge it
> into `.mcp.json["mcpServers"]["brain"]`, and write the file back.

Same regression as the previous example — the agent is now the mutation
surface, not the sanctioned binary. The interpolation, idempotency, and
legacy-key-removal logic that lives inside the sync CLI is reinvented
(badly) by the agent and drifts from the canonical implementation.

A re-call of `aide_info` after sync:

> Sync completed. Call `aide_info` again to confirm `brain.status` is
> now `ok` before continuing.

Trap. The brain MCP server in the running session was launched without
the brain entry; mutating `.mcp.json` mid-session updates disk state but
leaves the live brain server unwired. `aide_info` in the same session
reports disk state, not live-server state, and `status: ok` here is
false confidence — continuing the user's original request will hit a
brain server that is still operationally broken.

A hardcoded sync invocation:

> After scaffolding, run `cmd /c npx @aidemd-mcp/server sync` via Bash.

Breaks for local-dev installs (where the launcher is something like
`node D:/path/to/dist/index.js`) and for any custom binary path the
user has. The launcher must be recovered from
`.mcp.json["mcpServers"]["aide"]` at runtime so the same instructions
work uniformly across install shapes.

## References

- `.aide/handoff.aide` -- post-collapse architecture snapshot. Drew from intent #1 (canonical `.aide/config/brain.aide` path, user-owned-config boundary), intent #6 (hub-artifact bytes flow from `brain.aide` body sections, retired `*_TEMPLATE` constants — justifies dropping the second `aide_init` seeding step), intent #11 (no introspection of `mcpServerConfig.args` — anchors the no-retired-fields rule), intent #12 (four-state boot status set, `invalid-path` retired with `rootPath`), and intent #14 (MCP server never silently rewrites `.mcp.json`; sync CLI is the only mutation surface — anchors the agent-via-Bash recipe and the rejection of an `aide_sync` MCP tool).
- `.aide/intent.aide` -- root spec for the project. Drew from the `outcomes.desired` block defining `mcp-drift` as a hard halt, the `outcomes.undesired` rejection of MCP-runtime mutation of `.mcp.json` (justifies why the agent must invoke the binary rather than write the file directly), and the `.aide/config/` ownership-boundary outcome (path is the ownership signal, no per-file allowlist).
- `src/types/index.ts` (BrainState definition) -- authoritative tagged union for the four post-collapse boot states. Drew the exact field set (`status`, `name`, `hints`) the prompt's "gather state" passage must name, and confirmed that no state carries `vaultPath`/`rootPath`/`connector`/`entryFile`/`tools`.
- `src/types/index.ts` (BrainToolResult definition) -- authoritative shape returned by `aide_brain`. Confirmed Mode 1's "execute the instructions verbatim" prose remains correct: the result is just `status` + `instructions`, with no schema change forced on the prompt.
- `src/cli/sync/index.ts` -- authoritative sync CLI behavior. Drew the contract that sync reads `.aide/config/brain.aide`, writes the `brain` key in `.mcp.json` (and removes the legacy `obsidian` key), is idempotent, and is the only mutation surface — anchors the agent-invokes-the-binary recipe and confirms that the same binary handles both `no-mcp-entry` (fresh write) and `mcp-drift` (overwrite).
- `.claude/commands/aide/brain.md` -- current (stale, pre-collapse) skill prose. Used as the diff target — the bad-examples section names the exact phrasings in the current prompt that violate the post-collapse contract (the `invalid-path` branch, the `vaultPath` field reference, the legacy `.aide/brain.aide` path reference, the "run sync in your terminal" prose, the "Step 4 — Seed the vault directories" second `aide_init` call).
